## From C++

`cpp/` holds the same runtime in C++17 (`cpp/runtime.hpp`, header-only). It
reads the same json and runs the same graphs, and it too holds no numeric
logic. You need a C++17 compiler and CMake 3.24 or newer. Configuring
downloads the prebuilt onnxruntime release for your platform and
nlohmann/json, both pinned by SHA256:

```bash
cmake -S cpp -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build --config Release
build/validate            # reproduces */reference/ from C++; exits 1 on a mismatch
build/example             # a forecast, a step-by-step rollout, one policy command
```

On Windows the programs land in `build\Release\`. Run them from this
directory, or pass the package directory as the first argument to `validate`.
To use an onnxruntime you already have, pass
`-DONNXRUNTIME_ROOT=<unpacked release>`. An installed nlohmann/json 3.11+ is
used in preference to the download.

To use it in your own program, link `onnxruntime` and include `runtime.hpp`:

```cpp
#include "runtime.hpp"

nt::Model model("model");
nt::State state = model.warmup(y, u, d);    // each [history_steps, n], row-major
state = model.step(state, u_now, d_now);    // [nu], [nd] -> a new State
state.outputs;                              // [ny], in model.y_columns order

nt::Policy policy("policy");
std::vector<float> command = policy.act({{"y", y}, {"ymin", ymin}, {"ymax", ymax}, {"d", d}});
```

Every array is a flat `std::vector<float>` in physical units. Its columns
follow the order in `model.json` / `policy.json`, which `model.y_columns`,
`policy.inputs` and so on expose. The program loads `libonnxruntime` at run
time. If you ship a binary, put that library beside it or on the loader
path. `cpp/` is rewritten on every export, so copy the files out before you
change them.

## Checking it

`validate.py` reproduces each `*/reference/` fixture with this machine's
onnxruntime. Those fixtures were written by the **trained torch model** at
export time, not by these graphs, so a pass covers the whole chain: trained
model, export, runtime, platform. The tolerances in each `REFERENCE.json`
are 1e-3 of each column's normalisation scale, headroom for float
differences between machines; the export itself agreed with the trained
model much more tightly (`agreement_at_export`).

`validate.py` and `example.py` are written once and never overwritten by a
re-export: put project-specific checks at the end of `validate.py`.
